GUIDES = [
dict(slug='guide-set-design-for-camera.html', pillar='set-design',
 title='Designing a Set for Camera: Studio & Stage Design for Streamed Events | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How to design an event set that works on a stream: depth and layers, sightlines and camera positions, colour and contrast, branding placement, furniture and the mistakes that flatten a room on screen.',
 h1='Set design for camera: <span class="em">build for the lens, not the room.</span>', h1_plain='Set design for camera: build for the lens, not the room',
 lede='A stage that wows the delegates in the hall can look like a flat wall on a stream. The principles that make a set work through a camera.',
 related=['guide-led-vs-green-screen.html','guide-lighting-for-broadcast.html','guide-presenter-on-camera.html'],
 body='''
<p>A camera is a two-dimensional, narrow-angled, unforgiving eye. It flattens depth, exaggerates clutter and notices things a human in the room never would. Designing a set for a streamed or hybrid event means designing for that eye first, and the room second.</p>
<h2>Depth: layers beat backdrops</h2>
<p>The single biggest improvement to any set on camera is separation between the presenter and the background. A presenter standing a metre in front of a flat wall looks pasted on. Two to three metres of distance, with something between — a lit panel, a plant, a piece of furniture, a second layer of set — gives the camera depth to render. Lighting can then treat the presenter and the background separately, which is where the "broadcast look" comes from.</p>
<h2>Design to the camera positions</h2>
<p>Decide the shots before the set: a wide, a mid on the presenter, a two-shot for interviews, an over-the-shoulder for panels. Then place the set elements where those shots see them. Logos that sit perfectly centred in the wide vanish behind the presenter's head in the mid; a screen that's readable from the room is a blown-out rectangle on camera unless its brightness is set for the lens.</p>
<h2>Colour and contrast</h2>
<p>Mid-tones and muted colours read well; pure white and pure black are difficult (white burns out, black swallows detail). Avoid fine patterns — narrow stripes and small checks shimmer on camera (moiré). Give the presenter a colour that contrasts with the set: a navy suit against a navy backdrop is a floating head. Brand palettes designed for print often need calming for camera; our own brand navy works because it's lit with depth rather than used flat.</p>
<h2>Screens on set</h2>
<p>LED walls and monitors on set are wonderful when they're managed and awful when they aren't. Content on them must be designed for the camera's exposure, not for the room's eyes; typically the screen runs at 20–40% of its room brightness. Frame rates and refresh rates need matching to the camera to avoid flicker and banding. The <a href="guide-led-vs-green-screen.html">backdrop guide</a> goes deeper.</p>
<h2>Branding placement</h2>
<p>Think in shot sizes. The wide can carry a large brand element; the mid needs a smaller repeated element that survives beside a head; the close-up carries branding through lower thirds rather than set. A logo the audience sees in every shot, at the right size, is far more valuable than one enormous logo seen only in the opening wide.</p>
<h2>Furniture and human scale</h2>
<p>Low, wide seating photographs better than tall chairs — it keeps heads in frame and lets the camera see set behind. Tables hide hands and gestures; if you need one, keep it low and narrow. Presenters standing beat presenters sitting for energy. And leave space: a crowded set reads as clutter on camera even when it looks lush in the room.</p>
<h2>Practicalities that get missed</h2>
<ul><li>Cable routes and power for lights, screens and cameras, planned before the set is built.</li><li>Floor: matte, not glossy — reflections kill lighting.</li><li>A place for a presenter monitor and teleprompter that doesn't appear in shot.</li><li>Room for the camera positions themselves, including the wide, which needs distance.</li><li>Sound: hard flat surfaces make a set echo. Soft materials and broken-up walls help the audio as much as the picture.</li></ul>
<div class="callout"><p>Ask your set designer to draw the set from each camera position, not just from the audience. If they can't, involve the video HOD before the build.</p></div>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-led-vs-green-screen.html', pillar='set-design',
 title='LED Wall vs Green Screen vs Cyc: Choosing a Studio Backdrop for Streaming | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='The honest comparison of LED walls, green screen (chroma key) and physical cyc or built sets for virtual events: cost, look, presenter comfort, technical risk and when each is right.',
 h1='LED wall, green screen or built set? <span class="em">The honest comparison.</span>', h1_plain='LED wall, green screen or built set? The honest comparison',
 lede='Three ways to put something behind a presenter, three very different budgets and risks. Which one suits your event.',
 related=['guide-set-design-for-camera.html','guide-lighting-for-broadcast.html','guide-graphics-lower-thirds.html'],
 body='''
<p>Every studio brief eventually arrives at the same question: what goes behind the presenter? The answer decides the look, a large share of the budget, and how much can go wrong on the day. Here's how the three main options compare from the gallery's point of view.</p>
<h2>The built set or cyc</h2>
<p>A physical backdrop: a white or coloured cyclorama, a designed set of panels, furniture and lighting. <strong>Strengths:</strong> nothing to fail technically; presenters are comfortable in a real space; lighting creates genuine depth; easy to reconfigure with light colour. <strong>Weaknesses:</strong> what you see is what you get — changing the look means changing the physical set; branding is static unless you add screens. <strong>Cost:</strong> from a few hundred pounds for a lit cyc in an existing studio to several thousand for a bespoke build. Our own studio's white cyc is the workhorse for exactly this reason — it's the cheapest thing on the list that always works.</p>
<h2>Green screen (chroma key)</h2>
<p>The presenter stands in front of a green (or blue) backdrop and the gallery replaces it with any image or animation. <strong>Strengths:</strong> unlimited looks for almost no set cost; change the background per session or per client; great for branded virtual environments. <strong>Weaknesses:</strong> it must be lit evenly and the presenter separately, or the key has fringing and holes; fine hair, glasses and green ties are trouble; presenters are working in a featureless space and can't see the world they're apparently in; poor keys look unmistakably cheap. <strong>Cost:</strong> low on materials, but you're paying for the lighting skill and gallery time that make it invisible.</p>
<h2>The LED wall</h2>
<p>A wall of LED panels displaying content behind the presenter in real time: a designed virtual environment, live graphics, even a remote guest at scale. <strong>Strengths:</strong> the presenter sees the real background and reacts to it; camera and content are genuinely in the same space, so the depth and reflections are real; instantly rebrandable; spectacular in a wide shot. <strong>Weaknesses:</strong> cost — hire, structure, processing and content creation add up fast; technical care needed for moiré, refresh rates, colour matching and brightness; small panels look small on a wide, and big walls need a big room. <strong>Cost:</strong> from a couple of thousand pounds a day for a modest wall to five figures for a large one with content.</p>
<h2>Side by side</h2>
<table><tr><th></th><th>Built set / cyc</th><th>Green screen</th><th>LED wall</th></tr>
<tr><td>Look</td><td>Real, depends on lighting</td><td>Anything, depends on key quality</td><td>Anything, premium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Technical risk</td><td>Very low</td><td>Medium (lighting, edges)</td><td>Medium (processing, moiré)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Presenter comfort</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>High</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rebranding</td><td>Physical change</td><td>Instant</td><td>Instant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical day cost</td><td>£ – ££</td><td>£ – ££</td><td>£££ – ££££</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Interviews, town halls, podcasts</td><td>Branded virtual studios, multi-client days</td><td>Launches, flagship events, shows with big wides</td></tr></table>
<h2>How we'd choose</h2>
<p>A clear, single-brand event with talking heads: a well-lit built set or cyc, every time. A production company running several clients through one studio in a week: green screen, with a lighting rig set up once and left alone. A product launch or annual flagship where the wide shot is the hero image: LED wall, budgeted properly, with content designed for it. And a mix is common — a built set with an LED panel as one element gives most of the flexibility for much less of the cost.</p>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-lighting-for-broadcast.html', pillar='set-design',
 title='Lighting for Broadcast & Live Streaming: Studio and Stage Lighting Basics | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How to light presenters and sets for camera: key, fill and back light, colour temperature, LED panels vs stage fixtures, lighting the background separately, and fixing common problems.',
 h1='Lighting for camera: <span class="em">why the room looks bright and the stream looks dull.</span>', h1_plain='Lighting for camera: why the room looks bright and the stream looks dull',
 lede='A camera needs more, softer, better-placed light than a human eye. The three-point setup, colour temperature, and the fixes for the problems you\'ll actually see.',
 related=['guide-set-design-for-camera.html','guide-led-vs-green-screen.html','guide-presenter-on-camera.html'],
 body='''
<p>Human eyes are extraordinary at coping with bad light. Cameras aren't. A conference room that feels bright can produce a stream where the presenter is a dark shape against a burnt-out window. Lighting for camera is a discipline of its own, but the principles are learnable and most of the impact comes from a handful of decisions.</p>
<h2>Key, fill and back: the three-point setup</h2>
<p>The <strong>key</strong> is the main light on the presenter's face, placed off to one side at roughly 45 degrees and above eye level, so the face has shape. The <strong>fill</strong> is a softer light on the other side that lifts the shadows the key creates — how much fill decides how dramatic or flat the face looks. The <strong>back light</strong> (or hair light) comes from behind and above, catching the shoulders and hair and separating the presenter from the background. Miss the back light and presenters merge into the set; it's the light most people forget and the one that most looks "professional".</p>
<h2>Soft light is kind light</h2>
<p>Large, diffused sources — softboxes, LED panels through diffusion, bounced light — produce gentle shadows and flatter faces. Small hard sources produce sharp shadows, shiny foreheads and squinting. On a stream, softness is almost always right for the key and fill; a harder back light adds sparkle.</p>
<h2>Colour temperature: pick one and stick to it</h2>
<p>Daylight is bluish (around 5600K); tungsten and many venue lights are warm (around 3200K); office fluorescents are somewhere unpleasant in between. Mixed sources give a presenter a blue face on one side and an orange one on the other, and no camera setting fixes it. Choose a temperature, set all your fixtures to it, kill or gel the room lights you can't control, and white-balance the cameras to match.</p>
<h2>Light the background separately</h2>
<p>The "broadcast look" is a presenter lit for the face and a background lit for mood, independently. That's what makes coloured washes on a set work — the presenter stays natural while the wall goes brand-teal. It requires the separation described in <a href="guide-set-design-for-camera.html">set design</a> and a few extra fixtures aimed only at the set.</p>
<h2>Screens, windows and the exposure fight</h2>
<p>Anything self-luminous — an LED wall, a monitor, a window — is fighting the presenter for exposure. The camera can only expose for one. The fix is to bring the presenter's light level up to meet the screen, turn the screen's brightness down to meet the presenter, or both. For windows: blinds, or place the presenter so the window is the key light rather than the background.</p>
<h2>Fixtures for events</h2>
<p>Bi-colour LED panels are the event workhorse: adjustable colour temperature, dimmable, cool-running, battery-capable. Fresnels and stage fixtures are for larger stages. RGB tube lights and pixel bars have made coloured set lighting cheap and quick. What matters more than the brand is having enough of them and someone who knows where to put them.</p>
<h2>Fixing what you'll see on the monitor</h2>
<table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
<tr><td>Dark face, bright background</td><td>Exposed for the background</td><td>More key light, dim the background or window</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shiny forehead</td><td>Hard light source, warm room</td><td>Diffuse the key, powder, cooler room</td></tr>
<tr><td>Presenter blends into set</td><td>No back light</td><td>Add a back light behind and above</td></tr>
<tr><td>Half the face is blue</td><td>Mixed colour temperatures</td><td>Match all sources; gel or switch off strays</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flicker or banding on screens</td><td>Refresh/shutter mismatch</td><td>Adjust camera shutter; match refresh rates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Glasses glare</td><td>Key too low and frontal</td><td>Raise the key, angle it further off axis</td></tr></table>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-graphics-lower-thirds.html', pillar='set-design',
 title='On-Screen Graphics for Live Streams: Lower Thirds, Name Straps & Branding | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How to brand a live stream with graphics: lower thirds and name straps, holding slides, stings and idents, safe areas and legibility, animated vs static, and the graphics package to brief.',
 h1='Graphics: <span class="em">the branding the camera can\'t see.</span>', h1_plain='Graphics: the branding the camera can\'t see',
 lede='Lower thirds, holding slides, stings and overlays — the graphics package that makes a stream look like a broadcast, and how to brief one.',
 related=['guide-set-design-for-camera.html','guide-accessibility.html','guide-run-order.html'],
 body='''
<p>The set is what the camera sees. The graphics are everything the gallery lays on top — and on a stream they carry more of the branding, more of the information and more of the polish than the set ever can. A good graphics package is the difference between "a Zoom with cameras" and a broadcast.</p>
<h2>The package</h2>
<p>A complete event graphics package typically contains: an <strong>opening sting</strong> (5–10 seconds of animated ident); a <strong>holding loop</strong> for before the show and during breaks, with a clock or "back at 11:15"; <strong>lower thirds</strong> for every speaker and every session; <strong>full-screen slides</strong> for agenda, sponsors and Q&amp;A prompts; <strong>overlays</strong> for polls, social handles and captions; <strong>bugs</strong> — the small persistent logo in a corner; <strong>transitions</strong>; and a <strong>closing graphic</strong>. Built once, in the client's brand, it's reused across every session.</p>
<h2>Lower thirds: the workhorse</h2>
<p>A name strap should be on screen long enough to read twice (7–10 seconds), appear when a speaker starts and each time they return after a cutaway, and sit within the <strong>safe area</strong> — clear of the edges where phone players and platform UI crop. Name, role, organisation; keep it to two lines. Animate on and off; a graphic that pops on instantly looks amateur. Prepare every strap in advance from the run order; typing names live is how "Cheif Executive" happens.</p>
<h2>Legibility on a phone</h2>
<p>A meaningful share of your audience is watching on a phone in a small player. Text below 28px on a 1080p frame is unreadable there. High contrast — light text on a dark translucent panel, or vice versa — beats brand-perfect but faint. Test every graphic on a phone before show day.</p>
<h2>Holding slides and the "what's happening" problem</h2>
<p>When nothing is on screen, viewers assume the stream is broken. The holding loop exists so that there's always something: a branded slide, a countdown, "the next session starts at 14:00", music. The same graphic covers a technical fault — "we're experiencing a brief technical issue, back shortly" is far better than a frozen frame or black.</p>
<h2>Animated versus static</h2>
<p>Animated graphics look better and cost more. A middle path: animated sting and transitions (built once), static-but-well-designed lower thirds and slides (cheap to change). Most graphics can be driven from a spreadsheet or the run order, so a name change on the morning is a cell edit, not a designer.</p>
<h2>Accessibility and captions</h2>
<p>Leave room at the bottom of frame for captions — burned-in or platform-rendered — so lower thirds and captions don't collide. Our <a href="guide-accessibility.html">accessibility guide</a> covers the rest.</p>
<h2>What to brief the designer</h2>
<ul><li>Brand assets: logos (with clear space rules), fonts, colours, any motion guidelines.</li><li>The full list of speakers and sessions from the run order.</li><li>Sponsor logos and where they must appear.</li><li>Frame size (1920×1080) and safe areas.</li><li>Delivery format the gallery needs (usually PNG sequences, MOV with alpha, or native titles for the production software).</li><li>Deadline: 72 hours before show day, so it can be loaded and tested.</li></ul>
'''),

dict(slug='guide-presenter-on-camera.html', pillar='set-design',
 title='Presenting to Camera: Eyeline, Framing, Teleprompters & Delivery for Streamed Events | Virtual Studio Events',
 desc='How to help presenters and executives perform for a camera rather than a room: eyeline and lens, framing, using a teleprompter, energy and pace, wardrobe and the cues from the floor.',
 h1='Presenting to camera: <span class="em">a room of one.</span>', h1_plain='Presenting to camera: a room of one',
 lede='Executives who command a conference hall often freeze in front of a lens. How to set presenters up to look natural, read well and take direction on a streamed event.',
 related=['guide-speaker-prep.html','guide-lighting-for-broadcast.html','guide-set-design-for-camera.html'],
 body='''
<p>A stage gives a presenter an audience to play to. A camera gives them a black circle. The energy they'd naturally draw from a room has to be manufactured, and the technique — eyeline, framing, pace — is different from live presenting. Most of it can be taught in a 20-minute session before the show.</p>
<h2>Eyeline: talk to the lens</h2>
<p>The viewer is in the lens. A presenter who looks at the floor manager, the monitor or the room is, to the stream, looking away from every single viewer. Put a small mark on the camera, or a face-shaped sticker, and tell them to talk to that person. For hybrid events, agree the split: the presenter addresses the room for the room's moments and the lens for the stream's — and the gallery cuts accordingly.</p>
<h2>Framing and movement</h2>
<p>Presenters who wander drift out of frame and out of the lit area. Mark the floor: a spot for the opening, a spot for the screen, and the limits. Explain the shot sizes so they know when a gesture is in frame and when it isn't. Standing beats sitting for energy; leaning slightly forward reads as engagement.</p>
<h2>Teleprompters</h2>
<p>A teleprompter lets a presenter deliver a script to the lens without losing eyeline. It needs a script formatted for it (short lines, no dense paragraphs), an operator who follows the presenter's pace rather than forcing it, and a rehearsal — reading a prompter naturally is a skill. For executives who won't rehearse, bullet-point prompts on a confidence monitor beneath the lens are often better than a full script.</p>
<h2>Energy and pace</h2>
<p>Cameras drain energy; a delivery that feels slightly over the top to the presenter usually looks right on screen. Pace slower than conversation, pause after key points (the cut to the graphic needs a beat), and smile before the first word. Ask them to imagine one specific person watching on a laptop — it changes the delivery more than any other note.</p>
<h2>Wardrobe</h2>
<p>Solid mid-tones; no fine stripes, checks or tight patterns (moiré); nothing the same colour as the set; nothing pure white or pure black if it can be helped; no green in a green-screen studio. Matte fabrics. Remove lanyards and rattling jewellery — lav mics hear everything. Bring a spare shirt for a long day.</p>
<h2>Cues from the floor</h2>
<p>Agree a small set of signals with the floor manager: "you're on", "wrap up" (a rolling hand), "cut" (a flat hand), and the count into a VT. Tell presenters what will happen if a remote guest drops or the prompter fails — a presenter who knows the plan stays calm; one who doesn't improvises badly.</p>
<h2>The 20-minute prep session</h2>
<ol><li>Show them the shot on a monitor so they know what the viewer sees.</li><li>Mark the eyeline and the floor positions.</li><li>Run the first 60 seconds twice, with notes on pace and energy.</li><li>Practise the handovers: to a VT, to a remote guest, to Q&amp;A.</li><li>Explain the failure plan and the cues.</li></ol>
<div class="callout"><p>Every presenter, however senior, benefits from the 20 minutes. The ones who "don't need it" are the ones who most do.</p></div>
'''),
]
